1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,239
The Lucene module gives your site full-text search with no other service, because

2
00:00:06,239 --> 00:00:08,528
the index lives inside the site.

3
00:00:08,528 --> 00:00:13,225
In this video, you enable Lucene, create an index, learn what the search

4
00:00:13,225 --> 00:00:17,200
looks in, search the site, and see how servers stay in sync.

5
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,717
Searching needs an index:

6
00:00:23,717 --> 00:00:27,984
a copy of your text, organized so that words are found quickly.

7
00:00:27,984 --> 00:00:32,808
Elasticsearch and Azure AI Search keep that index in a separate service.

8
00:00:32,808 --> 00:00:35,220
Lucene keeps it inside your site.

9
00:00:35,970 --> 00:00:39,622
With the Lucene module, an index is a set of files in the

10
00:00:39,622 --> 00:00:43,680
folder of your tenant, the name Orchard Core gives to a site, on

11
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:45,419
the server that runs it.

12
00:00:45,419 --> 00:00:49,940
When content is published, unpublished or removed, the index is updated.

13
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:55,031
The module adds Lucene indexes to the Indexes page, options for how text

14
00:00:55,031 --> 00:00:57,925
is analyzed, and settings for what is indexed.

15
00:00:57,925 --> 00:01:02,210
The Search feature then uses an index for the search page of your site.

16
00:01:06,438 --> 00:01:09,559
Open Tools, Features, and search for Lucene.

17
00:01:09,559 --> 00:01:13,928
The Lucene module lists several features in the Search category.

18
00:01:14,403 --> 00:01:17,597
Lucene is the feature that provides indexes.

19
00:01:17,597 --> 00:01:24,241
Lucene (Obsolete) exists for backwards compatibility and enables Lucene, and Lucene Content Picker

20
00:01:24,241 --> 00:01:27,243
adds an editor for content picker fields.

21
00:01:27,721 --> 00:01:29,331
Select Enable on Lucene.

22
00:01:29,331 --> 00:01:32,176
A message confirms that the feature is enabled.

23
00:01:32,176 --> 00:01:37,061
Lucene depends on the Indexing feature, which provides the Indexes page you use next.

24
00:01:41,274 --> 00:01:44,415
Open Search, Indexes, and select Add index.

25
00:01:44,415 --> 00:01:49,735
The dialog lists the sources, and under Lucene, Content in Lucene indexes the

26
00:01:49,735 --> 00:01:51,850
content items of your site.

27
00:01:51,850 --> 00:01:53,324
Select Add on it.

28
00:01:53,776 --> 00:01:57,418
Index Name is the name of the index in Lucene itself, and it

29
00:01:57,418 --> 00:01:59,295
can't change after you save.

30
00:01:59,295 --> 00:02:01,061
Name is filled in from it.

31
00:02:01,061 --> 00:02:05,366
The search page and the Site Search settings refer to the index by Name.

32
00:02:05,826 --> 00:02:09,089
Choose the content types to index, at least one.

33
00:02:09,089 --> 00:02:11,990
Let's select Article, Blog Post, and Page.

34
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:17,006
Culture can limit the index to one culture, and Any culture indexes them all.

35
00:02:17,525 --> 00:02:21,943
Analyzer decides how text is split into the terms the index stores, and

36
00:02:21,943 --> 00:02:24,180
the default analyzer is selected.

37
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:29,745
Store Source Data keeps field values in the index for later retrieval, and stays unchecked.

38
00:02:30,197 --> 00:02:33,525
Query field names lists the fields a search looks in.

39
00:02:33,525 --> 00:02:38,432
The full-text field, which gathers the searchable text of each item, is selected.

40
00:02:38,432 --> 00:02:40,857
Leave the other settings as they are.

41
00:02:41,338 --> 00:02:45,337
Save creates the index and starts filling it in the background.

42
00:02:45,337 --> 00:02:49,742
A message confirms it, and the list shows the index with its provider,

43
00:02:49,742 --> 00:02:51,828
Lucene, and its type, Content.

44
00:02:56,038 --> 00:03:00,921
Under Design, Content Definition, Content Types, select Edit on Article.

45
00:03:00,921 --> 00:03:05,116
Its Full-text section decides what the full-text field holds:

46
00:03:05,116 --> 00:03:08,998
the display text, the body parts, or a custom full-text.

47
00:03:09,483 --> 00:03:13,527
Include display text and Include body parts are selected by default.

48
00:03:13,527 --> 00:03:17,353
That is why a search finds the title and the text of an article.

49
00:03:17,823 --> 00:03:21,128
Parts and fields have their own Lucene Index settings.

50
00:03:21,128 --> 00:03:25,149
On the Title part, Include this element in the index adds the value

51
00:03:25,149 --> 00:03:28,123
of that part to the index as a field of its own.

52
00:03:28,575 --> 00:03:33,023
Keyword indexes the value as a single token, and Stored lets you retrieve

53
00:03:33,023 --> 00:03:35,275
the original value from the index.

54
00:03:35,727 --> 00:03:40,237
Only people with the Manage Lucene Indexes permission see these settings.

55
00:03:40,701 --> 00:03:42,571
We do not save this change.

56
00:03:42,571 --> 00:03:46,821
The full-text field holds the title, and the search reads that field.

57
00:03:51,058 --> 00:03:55,662
The Search feature, enabled on the Features page like Lucene, adds a search

58
00:03:55,662 --> 00:03:57,140
page for each index.

59
00:03:57,140 --> 00:04:01,403
Its address is the address of your site, followed by the word search,

60
00:04:01,403 --> 00:04:03,278
a slash and the index name.

61
00:04:03,737 --> 00:04:07,368
On the page of the index we created, let's search for garden.

62
00:04:07,368 --> 00:04:11,757
Lucene looks the word up in the full-text field of every item in the index.

63
00:04:12,227 --> 00:04:16,380
The results are the content items that match, ordered by how well they

64
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:18,347
match, and shown as summaries.

65
00:04:18,825 --> 00:04:22,757
Visitors who are not signed in can search once the Anonymous role has

66
00:04:22,757 --> 00:04:26,795
the Query any index permission, or the permission Query 'search' Index.

67
00:04:27,323 --> 00:04:31,580
Without an index name in its address, the search page uses the default

68
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:34,213
index chosen in the Site Search settings.

69
00:04:38,413 --> 00:04:40,691
A site can run on several servers.

70
00:04:40,691 --> 00:04:44,679
Each server keeps its own Lucene files and its own record of how

71
00:04:44,679 --> 00:04:49,863
far it has indexed, so each index must learn about changes made on the other servers.

72
00:04:50,613 --> 00:04:53,544
Content changes are recorded in the database.

73
00:04:53,544 --> 00:04:58,488
A background task named Indexes Content Updater runs every minute and brings the

74
00:04:58,488 --> 00:04:59,926
indexes up to date.

75
00:04:59,926 --> 00:05:02,973
The Indexing Worker feature provides that task.

76
00:05:03,723 --> 00:05:06,621
The Features page also lists Lucene Worker.

77
00:05:06,621 --> 00:05:08,573
It adds no task of its own.

78
00:05:09,323 --> 00:05:12,615
A site on one server updates its index itself.

79
00:05:12,615 --> 00:05:17,553
A site on several servers relies on the task to reach the other servers.

80
00:05:19,553 --> 00:05:21,195
That's the Lucene module:

81
00:05:21,195 --> 00:05:25,805
full-text search with an index inside your site, and a search page that reads it.

82
00:05:25,805 --> 00:05:27,183
Thanks for watching.

